\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Haar averaging of bounded link observables}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{X=G^{E_s}} and #{\Gamma=G^{V_s}} be the configuration space and gauge group used to define the \vocabk{link Hilbert space}{fdip-007Z}, with #{G} finite, #{U(1)} or #{SU(2)}, and with normalized Haar measure. Let #{A} be the algebra of bounded Borel functions #{X\to\mathbb C} under pointwise product. For a closed subgroup #{H\subseteq\Gamma}, define
##{(P_Hf)(U)=\int_H f(h^{-1}\cdot U)\,d\mu_H(h).}
Then #{P_H} is a \vocabk{normalized conditional functional}{fdip-00KV} of #{A} onto the #{H}-invariant functions in #{A}. If #{H\subseteq\Gamma}, the gauge average of \ref{fdip-0083} satisfies #{P_\Gamma\circ P_H=P_\Gamma}.}

\proof{
\p{The configuration space is compact and metrizable, and the action is continuous. For bounded Borel #{f}, the map #{(h,U)\mapsto f(h^{-1}\cdot U)} is Borel on #{H\times X}. Integration against the Haar probability in the group variable therefore returns a Borel function of #{U}, and #{|P_Hf|\le\|f\|_\infty}. Left invariance of #{\mu_H} gives #{P_Hf(k^{-1}\cdot U)=P_Hf(U)} for every #{k\in H}. An invariant function factors out of the integrand, so #{P_H} fixes the invariant subalgebra and pulls an invariant function through the integral on either side. The unit is fixed because the measure is a probability.}

\p{Fubini applies because both measures are probabilities and #{f} is bounded. Right invariance of #{\mu_\Gamma} reindexes #{g\mapsto gh} inside
##{P_\Gamma P_Hf(U)
=\int_\Gamma\int_H f(h^{-1}g^{-1}\cdot U)\,d\mu_H(h)\,d\mu_\Gamma(g),}
and #{h^{-1}g^{-1}=(gh)^{-1}}. The inner integral over #{H} then collapses. The resulting identity is the raw integration identity of \ref{fdip-00KX} with every weight equal to #{1}.}

\p{On a bounded representative, the same integral is the orthogonal projection that defines the [physical Hilbert space](fdip-0083). The #{L^2} space itself is a different object. On #{U(1)}, a function comparable to #{|\theta|^{-1/3}} near the identity lies in #{L^2}, and its pointwise square does not. TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{ContRepresentation.haarAverageMap} is the integrated operator of a continuous representation on a complete normed space. It supplies the projection on that space, not the pointwise product of bounded Borel functions.}
}
